---
// /about-us/ — noindex until the company story is sourced.
//
// An about page is mostly company-history claims, and Coldstream's are not on file anywhere in
// this repo: founding year, ownership, crew size, how long each market has been open. The
// prototype's "25+ years" came from the same design comp as the fake phone numbers, so it is
// gated in data/claims.js with everything else.
//
// What is below is only what is verifiable today: three real offices, the services each market
// runs, and the three pre-approved claims. That is a real page, but it is not yet a page that
// earns the About search — so it carries a noindex and the build names what it needs.
import PageShell from "../layouts/PageShell.astro";
import { MARKET_LIST, NATIONAL, servicesFor } from "../data/markets.js";
import { CLAIMS } from "../data/claims.js";
import { organizationNode } from "../data/seo.js";
import TeamProfiles from "../components/TeamProfiles.astro";
import { TEAM } from "../data/team.js";

// The same Organization node the home page uses — see data/seo.js. It was a third hand-written
// copy until round 42, and the copies had already drifted.
const org = organizationNode();
---
<PageShell
  reviews={true}
  offer={true}
  title="About Coldstream Exteriors | Ohio & Missouri Roofers"
  description="Coldstream Exteriors runs three offices — Milford and Galloway in Ohio, and Geyer Road in St. Louis — with a local team in each. Licensed and insured."
  canonical="/about-us/"
  noindex={true}
  schema={[org]}
  eyebrow="About us"
  heading="Three metros. Our own crew in each."
  intro="We do roofs, siding, windows and gutters. We do them with our own people, out of a real office in each market — not a call centre with a map on the wall."
>
  {/* THE OWNERS, ABOVE EVERYTHING ELSE (owner brief 2026-08-25): Jon, Greg and Tyler, ported
      from the live about page — same info, same images. An about page whose first section is
      three real people is also the strongest possible answer to the page's noindex reason: the
      company story is no longer entirely unsourced, it is bylined. */}
  <TeamProfiles people={TEAM} />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What we do</h2>
      <p>
        Roofing, siding, windows and gutters — the whole outside of a house. Roofing is the core of
        the business and the rest grew around it, for a straightforward reason: water that gets
        past the roof shows up in the siding, and a gutter that cannot carry a downpour ends up in
        the basement. Treating the outside of a building as one system rather than four trades is
        the difference between fixing a symptom and fixing a cause.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also do commercial and multi-family work — flat and low-slope systems, coatings and
        restoration, and the scheduling that makes a roof replaceable while the building stays
        occupied.
      </p>

      <h2>Three companies, run locally</h2>
      <p>
        Coldstream runs as three local businesses rather than one brand with a map on the wall.
        Cincinnati, Columbus and St. Louis each have their own office, their own crew and their own
        phone number. The person who answers knows what the weather does to houses in that market,
        because they work there.
      </p>
      <div class="mkt-about">
        {MARKET_LIST.map((m) => (
          <div class="mkt-about-card">
            <h3><a href={`/${m.slug}/`}>{m.name}</a></h3>
            <p class="ma-meta">{m.office.street}, {m.office.city}, {m.office.state} {m.office.zip}</p>
            <p class="ma-meta"><a href={m.telHref}>{m.phone}</a> · {m.servedAreas.length} communities across {m.region}</p>
            <p class="ma-svc">{servicesFor(m).map((sv) => sv.label).join(" · ")}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>How a job actually runs</h2>
      <p>
        Every job starts with a free inspection. Someone climbs up, looks at what is actually
        happening, photographs it, and tells you what they found — including when a repair is the
        honest answer and a replacement is not. Then you get a written quote with a number on it,
        the scope spelled out and the materials named.
      </p>
      <p>
        The crew that quotes the job is the crew that does it. We do not subcontract, which is less
        a philosophy than a practical matter: when the person who wrote the scope is the person on
        the roof, nothing is lost between the two, and there is nobody to point at afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the end we walk it with you, run a magnet over the drive and take the debris with us.
        Our workmanship carries its own warranty and a claim on it goes to the same office that
        did the work.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will tell you that you may not want to hear</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>When a repair beats a replacement.</strong> A roof at the end of its life is a different conversation from a roof with a failed flashing detail, and the second one is far more common than the first.</li>
        <li><strong>When a claim will not go anywhere.</strong> Sudden storm damage is usually covered; age and wear usually are not. Filing regardless puts a claim on your record for nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>When gutter guards are not worth it.</strong> Under heavy tree cover they turn an annual clean into an occasional one. On an open lot they rarely pay for themselves.</li>
        <li><strong>When it can wait.</strong> Some things genuinely can, and saying so costs us a job and keeps a customer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Licensing, insurance and the warranty</h2>
      <p>
        We are licensed for the work we carry out in Ohio, Kentucky and Missouri, and we carry
        liability cover and workers' compensation — so both your property and the people on it are
        covered. We will send the certificates before you commit to anything; you should ask any
        contractor for them and be suspicious of one who hesitates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Materials carry their manufacturer's warranty, which is the manufacturer's promise about
        the product. Our workmanship carries ours, for 25 years, which is our promise about the
        installation. They are separate things and a contractor who blurs them is worth a second
        look.
      </p>

      {!CLAIMS.experience && (
        <div class="unsourced">
          <strong>Not yet on this page</strong>
          <p>
            The company history — when Coldstream started, who owns each market, how many people
            are on the crews and how many homes we have worked on — is not published here yet
            because none of it is on file in a form we can stand behind. It goes in when it is
            sourced, not before. That is also why this page is not currently in the search index.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
</PageShell>
<style>
  .mkt-about{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:16px;margin:22px 0 30px}
  .mkt-about-card{background:var(--cs-paper);border:1px solid var(--cs-line);border-left:4px solid var(--cs-primary);
    border-radius:11px;padding:18px 20px;transition:transform .18s ease-out,border-color .18s ease-out,box-shadow .18s ease-out}
  .mkt-about-card:hover{transform:translateY(-3px);border-color:var(--cs-steel);
    box-shadow:0 12px 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--cs-text) 10%, transparent)}
  .mkt-about-card h3{font-size:1.16rem;margin:0 0 7px}
  .mkt-about-card h3 a{color:var(--cs-text);text-decoration:none}
  .mkt-about-card h3 a:hover{color:var(--cs-primary-deep)}
  .ma-meta{margin:0 0 5px!important;font-size:.88rem!important;line-height:1.5!important}
  .ma-svc{margin:9px 0 0!important;font-size:.82rem!important;color:var(--cs-steel)!important}
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.mkt-about-card{transition:none}.mkt-about-card:hover{transform:none}}
</style>
